<resources>
    <!--
      These styles only ever paint WINDOWS: the system's starting window on a cold start, and the
      activity window's background until the first composition. Every colour after that comes
      from VeldtTheme (see Theme.kt). Which of the three a window gets is decided in one place,
      LaunchTheme.kt, from the user's Light/Dark/Follow-system choice.

      Theme.Veldt is the MANIFEST theme, so it has to be DayNight: the starting window is drawn
      before any app code runs, and follows either the system's night mode or, on API 31+, the
      per-app night mode LaunchTheme hands the system. A pinned Dark parent here showed a Light
      user a dark launch window for about 450 ms (finding 10).

      Theme.Veldt.Light and Theme.Veldt.Dark are what MainActivity sets before super.onCreate for
      an explicit choice, read from ThemeModeMirror because DataStore cannot be read
      synchronously. Their parents name the ground outright rather than inheriting DayNight, so the
      choice beats the system's night mode.

      statusBarColor is transparent on all three for the launch frame: MainActivity's
      enableEdgeToEdge() sets the runtime value, but the theme is what the frame is drawn with.

      ThemeSourceGuardTest pins all three parents. Change them together with it.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Veldt" parent="Theme.Material3.DynamicColors.DayNight">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.Veldt.Light" parent="Theme.Material3.DynamicColors.Light">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.Veldt.Dark" parent="Theme.Material3.DynamicColors.Dark">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>
</resources>
